const slug = 'kobo-bookmark-progress-sync-rebuild-2026';
const title = 'Kobo Rebuilds Bookmark and Reading Progress Sync 2026';
const description = 'Kobo rebuilt bookmark and reading progress sync from the ground up. Here is the one-time fix on e-readers and apps, and why multi-device readers care.';
const canonical = `https://converter-epub.com/blog/${slug}/`;
const pubDate = "2026-09-12";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonicalUrl={`https://converter-epub.com/blog/${slug}/`} ogType="article" datePublished={pubDate}>
  <main class="blog-post">
    <article itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article">
      <meta itemprop="headline" content={title} />
      <meta itemprop="description" content={description} />
      <meta itemprop="datePublished" content="2026-09-12" />
      <span itemprop="author" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Person">
        <span itemprop="name">Jason Mai</span>
      </span>

      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-09-12">Published September 12, 2026</time></p>

      <p>
        If you have ever finished three chapters on your phone during a commute, opened your Kobo
        e-reader that evening, and found yourself back at the cover, you already know the problem
        Rakuten Kobo announced a fix for this week. On September 11, 2026, Kobo CEO Michael Tamblyn
        took to Bluesky to say the company had torn down and rebuilt its syncing system for bookmarks
        and reading progress — the plumbing that keeps your position in a book consistent across an
        e-reader, a phone app, and a tablet.
      </p>

      <p>
        The admission is unusually blunt for a hardware executive, which is what makes it worth
        paying attention to. As reported by
        <a href="https://goodereader.com/blog/kobo-ereader-news/kobo-fixes-the-e-book-syncing-problem" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Good e-Reader on September 11, 2026</a>,
        Tamblyn wrote: "We know some of you have been driven slowly crazy by the way your reading
        progress on the Kobo app doesn't always correctly sync with your e-reader or your e-reader to
        your app. Annoying. Bad experience. No one liked it. So we rebuilt the entire bookmarking and
        progress syncing system from the ground up."
      </p>

      <p>
        For anyone who reads across more than one device, this is a bigger deal than a firmware
        changelog entry usually is. Progress sync is the invisible feature that makes a multi-device
        reading habit work at all. When it drifts, readers quietly start trusting one device and
        ignoring the others — and a sideloaded EPUB library becomes harder to justify.
      </p>

      <h2>What Actually Changed, In Plain Terms</h2>

      <p>
        Kobo's syncing architecture was built for an earlier version of the company. As Tamblyn
        framed it in the same post, the company grew from "buy a book on your e-reader and read it"
        into a portfolio of devices released nearly every year, plus a suite of mobile apps and a
        cloud reader. The old system, in his words, "was bursting at the seams," and new books and
        reading progress stopped behaving predictably.
      </p>

      <p>
        The important caveat is the one Tamblyn volunteered up front, and it is worth repeating
        because it dominates the comments on every story about this issue: the bug only affected
        people who read on two or more devices. "First off, this only matters if you are an e-reader
        and an app user. Or a multi-ereader user," he wrote. "If you aren't trying to keep two
        devices in sync, this hasn't been a problem for you."
      </p>

      <p>
        That framing matters for how you evaluate the announcement. Single-device readers will
        notice nothing, which is why the problem never showed up as a headline complaint on
        mainstream review sites. But the population of readers who split time between an e-reader and
        a phone screen is exactly the population Kobo and its competitors are fighting for, and it is
        exactly the population that has been loudest in Kobo's community channels.
      </p>

      <h2>The One-Time Fix: Two Steps Per Device</h2>

      <p>
        The good news is that the repair is not a gradual rollout or a wait-for-your-firmware
        situation. It is a manual reseed you run once, and the instructions differ slightly depending
        on whether you are fixing an e-reader or a mobile app.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>On a Kobo e-reader:</strong> go to <em>Settings → Device Information → Repair Your
          Kobo Account</em>, and run the repair.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>In the Kobo mobile app</strong> (iOS or Android): sign out of your account and then
          sign back in.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Repeat on every device</strong> where you are signed in — the e-reader, the phone,
          the tablet, and any secondary e-reader.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        That last step is the one people skip, and it is the one that determines whether the fix
        sticks. Tamblyn was explicit about it: "You have to do this on all devices you're signed in
        to, but you only have to do it once; after that, everything will stay synced from now on."
      </p>

      <p>
        A reasonable reading of "from now on" is that the reseed forces each device to renegotiate
        its state against the rebuilt server-side system rather than trusting a cached local sync
        token. If you repair the e-reader but leave the phone on the old cached state, you have
        rebuilt one end of the bridge and left the other end on the old foundation — which is exactly
        the scenario that produces the "it worked for two days and then broke again" complaint.
      </p>

      <h2>What to Check Before and After You Repair</h2>

      <p>
        Sync is a silent feature, so it is worth doing a small amount of bookkeeping rather than
        assuming the repair worked. Before you start, open the book you are mid-way through on your
        primary device and note the chapter and approximate percentage. Do the same on the second
        device. If the two positions already disagree, you have confirmed the bug and you now have a
        baseline to test against.
      </p>

      <p>
        After the one-time repair on all devices, open the same book on each one in turn and let it
        sit connected to Wi-Fi for a moment. Then jump to a distinctive location — the start of a
        chapter is easiest to verify — and check whether the other device picks it up on its next
        sync check. Kobo e-readers sync on Wi-Fi checks rather than continuously, so give the second
        device a minute or two and a network connection before concluding anything.
      </p>

      <h3>Books with unusual metadata can still misbehave</h3>

      <p>
        One thing the rebuild does not change is metadata quality, and this is where a lot of
        sideloaded libraries get into trouble. Progress tracking depends on the device agreeing with
        the server about <em>which book</em> a position belongs to. If a file was converted from
        another format and arrived without a clean identifier — no ISBN, no consistent title and
        author strings, no stable internal identifier — the two devices can be reading byte-identical
        text while the service treats them as different books.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is a file-hygiene problem rather than a sync problem, and it is worth fixing separately.
        If you manage a large library, the workflow described in our
        <a href="/blog/calibre-edit-epub-metadata-2026/">Calibre EPUB metadata guide</a> covers
        cleaning up title, author, and series fields so that every device reports the same book
        identity. Readers who convert formats regularly will also want to check that their
        converted files carry the original identifier forward — our guide to
        <a href="/blog/epub-to-kindle-formatting-2026/">EPUB formatting and conversion</a> walks
        through what should survive a conversion and what routinely gets dropped.
      </p>

      <h3>Where the Kobo rebuild sits next to StoryGraph</h3>

      <p>
        It is easy to confuse this announcement with Kobo's StoryGraph integration, which we covered
        when it launched earlier this year in our
        <a href="/blog/kobo-storygraph-integration-2026/">Kobo StoryGraph integration guide</a>.
        They are different systems solving different problems. StoryGraph receives a record of books
        you have <em>finished</em> — title, author, reading duration, date finished — and pushes that
        outward so your reading stats populate automatically. It is explicitly one-way, it fires on
        completion rather than continuously, and it does not carry your current page position.
      </p>

      <p>
        The rebuilt system here is the opposite direction and a much finer grain: it is Kobo's own
        internal bookkeeping of <em>where you are right now</em> inside a book, plus your bookmarks
        and highlights, shared between devices you own. One is a social reading log; the other is
        the reason you do not lose your place. An active Kobo user with a StoryGraph account depends
        on both, and the two failing independently is exactly why the confusion happens.
      </p>

      <h2>Why Progress Sync Is Worth Caring About</h2>

      <p>
        The strategic backdrop is straightforward. Amazon's Kindle ecosystem keeps reading position
        in sync through WhisperSync, and it is one of the few features that genuinely locks readers
        in — losing your place across devices is annoying enough that people tolerate it rather than
        switch. Kobo has spent 2026 making a visible play for readers who want a more open ecosystem:
        expanded retail distribution at Best Buy in the United States, a firmware line that now
        ships EPUB-adjacent improvements and StoryGraph support, and a hardware lineup that now
        includes a genuine pocket-sized competitor to the Kindle.
      </p>

      <p>
        None of that matters if the basics are unreliable. An open ecosystem that lets you carry your
        EPUB library between devices is a strong pitch, but the pitch assumes the software layer
        holding the reading experience together works quietly in the background. A syncing system
        that loses your place is a liability that no amount of format openness offsets, because the
        friction lands on the reader every single day rather than once at purchase.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is also a maintenance dimension most people never think about. A broken sync stack
        makes a device feel disposable, which in turn makes people less willing to invest in keeping
        a proper library. If your reading position is unreliable, the file you own becomes
        interchangeable with a rented stream. Readers who care about
        <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">building a DRM-free EPUB library</a> specifically
        care about this distinction, because a library only has value if you actually keep using the
        devices that read it.
      </p>

      <h2>What This Signals for the Rest of 2026</h2>

      <p>
        Announcing a backend rebuild through a CEO's personal social account, with plain language
        instead of a press release, tells you Kobo treats this as a trust repair rather than a
        feature launch. That is the right instinct. Sync bugs are the kind of thing that never
        generate a dramatic headline but quietly erode confidence in an entire platform, and they are
        disproportionately damaging to a company whose pitch is "your books, on your devices, in an
        open format."
      </p>

      <p>
        For readers, the practical takeaway is small and immediate: if you split your reading between
        a Kobo e-reader and the Kobo app, spend five minutes running the repair on every signed-in
        device today. It is a two-tap fix on the e-reader and a sign-out/sign-in on the phone. Then
        test it with the book you are currently reading, in the middle of a chapter, where a
        mismatch is obvious. Five minutes now is a better trade than discovering at bedtime that you
        have lost forty pages of progress.
      </p>

      <p>
        For everyone else — single-device readers whose progress never drifted — the news is that a
        quiet, invisible weakness in the Kobo platform has been addressed before it became visible
        to you. That is the least dramatic possible outcome, and it is the one you want.
      </p>

      <section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Who was actually affected by the Kobo sync bug?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>
                Readers who used more than one device. Kobo's CEO stated directly that the problem
                only mattered to people using an e-reader and an app together, or two e-readers.
                If you only ever read on a single Kobo device, you were not experiencing the bug and
                you will notice no change after the rebuild.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">How do I fix Kobo sync on my e-reader?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>
                Go to Settings, then Device Information, then choose Repair Your Kobo Account. This
                is a one-time action. You must repeat the equivalent fix on every other device you
                are signed in to, or the devices can fall out of sync again.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">How do I fix it in the Kobo mobile app?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>
                Sign out of your Kobo account in the app and then sign back in. That is the whole
                procedure for the app side. Do it once, and do it on every device where you are
                signed in — phone, tablet, and any second e-reader.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Will the repair delete my bookmarks or reading progress?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>
                The procedure is a renegotiation against the rebuilt system rather than a wipe, and
                Kobo presents it as a fix rather than a reset. As always with anything touching sync
                state, it is sensible to note where you are in your current book before you start, so
                you can restore your position manually if one device comes back to the wrong spot.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Is this the same as the Kobo StoryGraph integration?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>
                No. StoryGraph receives a record of books you finish so your reading stats populate
                automatically, and it does not track your current page. The rebuilt system covered
                here tracks bookmarks, highlights, and exactly where you are inside a book across
                your own devices. They are separate systems that can fail independently.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Do I need to update my Kobo firmware for the sync fix?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>
                The fix is server-side plus a manual reseed, not a firmware version you have to wait
                for. Kobo's instructions describe the repair as an action you take in settings on the
                e-reader and by signing out and back in on apps. Keeping your device on the current
                firmware line is still good practice for other reasons, including the bookstore and
                StoryGraph groundwork shipped in recent updates.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
